Article 6-2 (Labor inspectors) (1) Labor inspectors appointed under the Labor Standards Act shall perform the duties of senior judicial police officers in relation to offenses prescribed by the following Acts committed within their jurisdiction: <Amended on May 4, 2010; Jun. 8, 2010; Apr. 30, 2019; Jun. 15, 2021; Jan. 4, 2022>
  1. Labor Standards Act;
  2. Minimum Wage Act;
  3. Equal Employment Opportunity and Work-Family Balance Assistance Act;
  4. Wage Claim Guarantee Act;
  5. Occupational Safety and Health Act;
  6. Act on the Prevention of Pneumoconiosis and Protection of Workers Suffering from Pneumoconiosis;
  7. Trade Union and Labor Relations Adjustment Act;
  8. Act on the Establishment and Operation of Teachers' Unions;
  9. Act on the Promotion of Workers' Participation and Cooperation;
  10. Framework Act on Labor Welfare;
  11. Act on the Employment Improvement of Construction Workers;
  12. Act on the Protection of Temporary Agency Workers;
  13. Act on the Guarantee of Workers' Retirement Benefits;
  14. Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public Officials' Labor Unions;
  15. Act on the Protection of Fixed-Term and Part-Time Workers;
  16. Act on Prohibition of Age Discrimination in Employment and Elderly Employment Promotion.
  17. Act on the Employment Improvement of Domestic Workers;
  18. Serious Accidents Punishment Act (only Articles 6 and 7 shall apply);
  19. Industrial Accident Compensation Insurance Act (only Article 127(3)3 shall apply).
  (2) Persons designated by the chief prosecutor of the district prosecutors’ office having jurisdiction over the posts of such persons, on the recommendation of the heads of agencies with which such persons are affiliated, from among State public officials of Grade VIII and IX who work for local employment and labor offices and local employment and labor branch offices and substations thereof and perform the duties of labor supervision, labor management cooperation, industrial safety, protection of working women, etc. shall perform the duties of junior judicial police officers regarding crimes under paragraph (1). <Amended on Jun. 4, 2010>
  (3) Seafarers’ labor supervisors appointed under the Seafarers Act shall perform the duties of senior judicial police officers in relation to offenses prescribed in the Seafarers Act or Labor Standard Act committed by ship owners and seafarers within their jurisdiction.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 13, 2008]

Article 7 (Captains and seamen) (1) Captains shall perform the duties of senior judicial police officers in relation to offenses committed on a seagoing ship (that which is of a gross tonnage of 20 or more tons or of 36 or more cubic meters of loadage and has a coastline route or farther as its navigation area), and a person designated by the captain from among purser or seamen of deck, engine and clerical department shall perform the duties of junior judicial police officers.
  (2) Airline pilots and flight attendants shall perform the duties of senior judicial police officers and junior judicial police officers in relation to offenses committed on an airplane by applying paragraph (1) mutatis mutandis.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 13, 2008]

Article 7-2 (Executive officers and employees of the Korea National Park Service) Among executive officers or employees working for the Korea National Park Service or branch offices thereof and designated by the chief prosecutor of the district prosecutors’ office having jurisdiction over the their post on the recommendation of the chairperson of Korea National Park Service, the executive officers of the Korea National Park Service and the heads of branch offices thereof shall perform the duties of senior judicial police officers in relation to flagrant offenders of subparagraphs 11, 12, 15, 17, 19 through 21, 23 through 25, 27 through 29, 32, 36 and 37 of Article 3(1) of the Punishment of Minor Offenses Act, among offenses prescribed in Article 6(1) of the same Act, committed in park areas, and employees shall perform the duties of junior judicial police officers in relation to such offenses. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 21, 2012; Oct. 16, 2018>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 13, 2008]
  [Title Amended on Oct. 16, 2018]

Article 7-3 (Staff members of Financial Supervisory Service) (1) Staff members of the Financial Supervisory Service or branches or local offices thereof, appointed by the chief prosecutor of the district prosecutors’ office having jurisdiction over their office, upon the recommendation of the Chairman of the Financial Services Commission, shall perform the duties of senior judicial police officers in relation to offenses prescribed in the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act if they fall under the following subparagraphs, and the other such employees not falling under the following subparagraphs shall perform the duties of junior judicial police officers in relation to such offenses:
  1. Staff members of Grade IV or higher;
  2. Staff members of Grade V recommended by the Chairman of the Financial Services Commission as eligible for the duties of senior judicial police officers.
  (2) When making recommendations under paragraph (1), the Chairman of the Financial Services Commission shall consult with the Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 11, 2015]

Article 8 Deleted. <Dec. 15, 2020>

Article 9 (Military judicial police officers) (1) Military judicial police officers pursuant to subparagraph 1 of Article 43 or subparagraph 1 of Article 46 of the Military Court Act who are designated by the chief prosecutor of district prosecutors’ office shall perform the duties of judicial police officers in relation to offenses prescribed by the Act on Special Measures for the Crimes Involved with Military Supplies.
  (2) Military judicial police officers under subparagraph 2 of Article 43 or subparagraph 2 of Article 46 of the Military Court Act who are designated by the chief prosecutor of district prosecutors’ office shall perform the duties of judicial police officers in relation to offenses prescribed by the Military Secret Protection Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 13, 2008]

Article 10 (Autonomous police public officials) Autonomous police chiefs, autonomous police assistant commissioner, autonomous police commanders, autonomous police majors, autonomous police captains and autonomous police lieutenants pursuant to the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City shall perform the duties of senior judicial police officers in relation to offenses prescribed in subparagraphs 5 (applicable only to the jurisdiction of the persons falling under subparagraphs 6 and 7 of Article 5), 6, 7, 11, 13, 15, 18, 19, 21, 22, 24, 25, 28, 29, 31, 32, and 41 through 46 of Article 6 of this Act, Articles 471 and 473 of the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City and Articles 477 and 478 of the same Act in relation thereto, and autonomous police sergeants, autonomous police detectives and autonomous police officers shall perform the duties of junior judicial police officers in relation to such offenses. <Amended on May 4, 2010; Jul. 24, 2015; Aug. 11, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 13, 2008]

Article 11 (References to criminal records and investigation records) For the purposes of designating or verifying eligibility of judicial police officers and military judicial police officers as prescribed in this Act, the chief prosecutor of the district prosecutors’ office may refer to criminal records and investigation records under Article 6 of the Act on the Lapse of Criminal Sentences.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 11, 2015]

Article 12 (Establishment of department exclusively for duties of judicial police officers) For the effective implementation of the duties of judicial police officers, the head of the agency with which judicial police officers under this Act are affiliated may set up a department exclusively for such duties.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 11, 2015]

